<!-- ==========================================================================
     PILGRIM GUIDELINES, DO'S & DON'TS & ACCORDION FAQ
     ========================================================================== -->
<section class="section-padding" id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="text-center">
      <span class="section-badge"><i class="fa-solid fa-circle-question"></i> Essential Guidelines</span>
      <h2 class="section-title" id="faq-heading">Pilgrim Guidelines <span>& Frequent Questions</span></h2>
      <p class="section-subtitle">
        Everything you need to know for an auspicious, respectful, and hassle-free pilgrimage to Naimisharanya Dham.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-grid">
      <!-- Left: Guidelines Boxes -->
      <div>
        <div class="guideline-box">
          <h4><i class="fa-solid fa-water" style="color:var(--gold);"></i> Chakra Tirtha Snan Etiquette</h4>
          <p>
            Take a holy dip with reverence. Enter through designated paved ghats. Chanting the Gayatri Mantra or Mahamrityunjay Mantra during snan enhances spiritual merit manifold.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="guideline-box">
          <h4><i class="fa-solid fa-shirt" style="color:var(--gold);"></i> Temple Dress Code</h4>
          <p>
            Modest traditional attire is recommended. For men: Dhoti-Kurta or traditional pants. For women: Sarees, Salwar Kameez or modest attire. Avoid leather items inside inner sanctums.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="guideline-box">
          <h4><i class="fa-solid fa-calendar-days" style="color:var(--gold);"></i> Best Season to Visit</h4>
          <p>
            <strong>October to March</strong> offers pleasant weather. The most spiritually potent times are <strong>Somvati Amavasya</strong>, <strong>Chaitra & Ashwin Navratri</strong>, and the <strong>Phalgun 84 Kosi Parikrama</strong>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Right: Interactive Accordion FAQs -->
      <div>
        <div class="faq-item active">
          <button class="faq-question" type="button">
            <span>1. What is the legend behind Chakra Tirtha?</span>
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down"></i>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            When 88,000 sages requested Lord Brahma to indicate a sanctified land where Kali Yuga's negative influences cannot penetrate, Brahma released His celestial wheel (Manomaya Chakra). The spot where its rim (Nemi) fell became known as Naimisharanya, and the circular water basin formed is the celebrated Chakra Tirtha.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" type="button">
            <span>2. Can rituals like Pind Daan and Pitru Tarpan be done here?</span>
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down"></i>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            Yes, Naimisharanya is considered on par with Gaya and Brahma Kapal for Pitru Karya. Performing tarpan and pind daan at Chakra Tirtha or Gomti Ghat is believed to bestow eternal peace upon ancestors.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" type="button">
            <span>3. Are clean stay options and Dharamshalas available?</span>
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down"></i>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            Yes, numerous options exist including UPSTDC Rahi Tourist Bungalow, Sri Ram Dharamshala, Kali Peeth Guest House, and multiple ashrams. You can book authorized rooms directly through our booking form or helpline.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" type="button">
            <span>4. How long is the famous 84 Kosi Parikrama?</span>
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down"></i>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            The 84 Kosi Parikrama spans approximately 250 kilometers around the entire holy perimeter covering Sitapur and Hardoi districts. It commences every year on Phalgun Shukla Pratipada and culminates on Phalgun Purnima with holy snan at Chakra Tirtha.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" type="button">
            <span>5. Are VIP darshan or wheelchair facilities available?</span>
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down"></i>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            Yes, for senior citizens and differently-abled devotees, ramp access is available at major temple entrances, and our Teerth Purohit Sewaks can arrange personalized assistance upon advance booking.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

